gf180mcu_ocd_ip__por

Power-on-reset circuit for GF180MCU for a 3.3V power supply
-----------------------------------------------------------
Specifications:
Operating voltage:  3.3V (down to 1.8V)
Reset delay time:   40ms (20ms at 1.8V)

Inputs:
	"ena" is a circuit enable and normally should be
		tied to the power supply, although it can be
		driven by the 5V PoR output in a dual-voltage
		system.

Outputs:
	"por_unbuf" is for testing and should not be used
	"porb" is low during the initial power-up and
		high after the delay period
	"por" is high during the initial power-up and
		low after the delay period.

Normally "porb" is used to drive an internal chip digital
reset because it has clean low and high states, whereas
"por" follows the supply voltage ramp at initial power-up.

History:  The Caravel harness chip design that Efabless made
for the Open MPW shuttle run featured a PoR circuit with a
simple timer based on a trickle current onto a capacitor to
set an approximatly 15ms delay between the 5V power supply
being turned on and the power-on-reset signal being forced
low.

Since Wafer.Space took over the GF180MCU shuttle runs in 2025,
more emphasis has been placed on developing IP for 3.3V
operation, including the Open Circuit Design dual-voltage
padframe I/O library, the 3.3V SRAM library, and the Avalon
Semiconductors 3.3V standard cell library.

The original PoR was calibrated for 5V operation, and when
given a 3.3V supply, the delay time is approximately 14 to
16 seconds, which is inappropriate for typical use.  The
fact that the PoR is so sensitive to all external conditions
was the impetus for creating this version.  This version of
the PoR is not only calibrated for use at 3.3V, and features
all 3.3V devices, but it also features a regulated voltage
which sets an internal bias to 1.2V independent of the
power supply, so that the reset time of 40ms is relatively
independent of the power supply.

This specific version is self-calibrated which makes it more
sensitive to the power supply level;  it can be improved by
replacing the resistor/transistor stack at the "inn" input
of the amplifier with a bandgap, since the circuit is designed
for a value of 1.2V.

Power supplies with an extremely long rise time (> 0.1s) will
cause the reset to trip at a low voltage (about 1.5V), which
should still be sufficient for a proper reset.

In a dual-voltage environment, this circuit should always be
connected to 3.3V.  If a reset is needed for the 5V domain,
use the original 5V PoR; the 5V PoR output can then feed the
"ena" enable input to the 3.3V PoR.  Otherwise, the "ena" is
for testing purposes only and would normally be tied to the
3.3V power rail.
